2013-09-20 05:04 — Jonadab the Unsightly One:
> On Thursday, September 19, 2013 1:02:44 PM UTC-4, James wrote:
>> And the lance is very useful as a ranged weapon.  
> I haven't yet managed to figure out how.  The problem is,
> pretty much every single monster you want to use ranged
> weapons on falls into one of two categories:
> 
> 1. It barely ever moves at all and is only dangerous
>    when attacked, so *any* ranged weapon will work,
>    even rocks.  Floating eyes, blue jellies, red and
>    brown molds, etc. all fall into this category.  It's
>    good to use your lance on them, but only because it's
>    a way to use (and therefore train) the lance.  The
>    ability to kill these monsters is not valid as an 
>    argument in favor of the lance, because it only makes
>    sense to use a lance to kill them if you have already
>    decided to make training your lance skill a goal.
> 
> 2. It does move, and so consequently you can only get
>    in (if you're lucky) one hit with the lance, and then
>    it's no longer exactly the right distance away.  Every
>    polearm has this problem.
AFAIK, there's a technique called jousting that — at high luck and skill
— seems like the best of both worlds.  I've never used it, but read
about it on the wiki.

>> You've got a lot of apples and carrots to start with, which
>> helps.  
> Even if you can find enough other food for yourself that
> you can afford to spend the carrots and apples on the pet,
> you'd still have to remember to babysit your pet and check
> *constantly* to see if it's hungry.
Praying when weak gives you a relatively reliant source of — well, not
exactly food, but you won't starve.  Unless you need to pray for
something else, of course.

>> Food isn't usually a problem, unless you're casting
>> a lot of spells (which you won't be, because until 
>> you find dragon scales and enchant them, you'll
>> need metal armor anyway).
> Knights in my experience are more likely to have hunger
> problems than a couple of the other strong roles (e.g.,
> Samurai), but no worse than Valkyrie and not nearly as
> bad as the weak roles (Wizard, Tourist, etc.)
Food definitely isn't a problem for the tourist, which I know rather
well.  He starts with loads of food, enough to retreat and heal many
times.  If lucky, he might even have some tins of spinach which partly
offset the strength penalty.

